{
  "correction_rules": [
    "Strong positive bias beyond ~10–15 day horizon: model stays in 70–74 range while realized prices trend down into low/mid-60s. Apply a horizon-increasing downward bias correction (small in days 1–7, large in days 15–30).",
    "Missed regime shift / mean reversion after local highs: when recent history includes a spike to ~71–73, the model extrapolates strength; realized path mean-reverts and often sells off. After any 2–3 day upswing or print >~72, damp the forecast slope and pull levels back toward the 20–30d mean.",
    "Overshoot persistence error: model forecasts are too smooth and too persistent (keeps elevated plateau), while outcomes show a sharp step-down around ~day 16–22. Introduce a decay term so deviations from the recent mean shrink faster with horizon.",
    "Downside tail underestimation: model rarely projects prices <~69 even when realized drops to ~63–66. Add a conditional lower-tail adjustment: if momentum is negative or volatility rises, widen downside and shift distribution lower for days 10–30.",
    "Turning-point lag: model reacts late to emerging downtrends (keeps rising/flat forecasts even as realized begins falling). Use recent 5–10d momentum as a sign filter—if momentum ≤ 0, cap any upward drift in the forecast and bias next 2–4 weeks downward."
  ]
}
